{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Harvest sits northwest of Huntsville, straddling the border of Madison and Limestone counties. If you're on Highway 53, near Capshaw Road, or out toward Toney, you're in our regular service area. We're just 11 miles away — that's closer than driving across Huntsville.</p>

<p>Harvest has grown fast. Really fast. What used to be farmland along Highway 53 and the Jeff Road corridor is now subdivisions. The community has essentially doubled in the past 20 years, and growth continues.</p>

<p>That growth pattern creates a specific gutter landscape:</p>

<p><strong>The new subdivisions (2005-present):</strong> Builder-grade gutters that are already showing their age. Thin aluminum, seams that leak, undersized for our storms. If your home is 8-15 years old with original gutters, you're likely in the replacement window.</p>

<p><strong>The established neighborhoods (1980s-early 2000s):</strong> Better-quality construction in many cases, but aging systems. Sectional gutters with 20-30+ years of wear. Fascia boards that may have hidden damage.</p>

<p><strong>The original Harvest properties:</strong> Older homes, some with character and history. Often undersized gutters from an era before modern drainage standards. Mature trees that drop serious debris.</p>

<p>Harvest also has history with severe weather. The 1974 and 2011 tornado outbreaks both affected this area. Residents here understand that storms are serious, and drainage systems need to perform when weather gets intense.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Quick reference for Harvest homeowners:</p>

<p><strong>Know your home's era:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>2010s-present:</strong> Builder-grade gutters — watch for seam leaks, overflow during moderate rain, sagging. Replacement window is 8-12 years.</li>
<li><strong>1990s-2000s:</strong> Often better construction, but systems are 20-30+ years old. Full replacement usually makes more sense than repair.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-1990:</strong> May have undersized systems. Upgrading to 6-inch gutters is worth considering.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>The tree factor:</strong> Established Harvest neighborhoods have mature trees. Newer subdivisions are getting there. Either way, debris management is part of gutter life here:</p>
<ul>
<li>Clean twice yearly minimum (more with heavy tree coverage)</li>
<li>Consider gutter guards if you're tired of ladder climbing</li>
<li>Watch for pine needles — they slip through standard guards and create dense clogs</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Storm season prep (March):</strong> Clear gutters before April. Harvest's location in the Tennessee Valley corridor means exposure to severe weather. Make sure your system is ready.</p>

<p><strong>Growth-related drainage:</strong> As Harvest continues developing, neighborhood drainage patterns change. New subdivisions uphill from established homes can increase runoff onto older properties. If you've noticed more water issues in recent years, upstream development may be why.</p>

<p><strong>When to call:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Gutters overflow during normal storms (not just heavy ones)</li>
<li>Seam leaks visible during rain</li>
<li>Sagging sections between hangers</li>
<li>Water pooling near foundation</li>
</ul>

